From: Jun-Hyuk Kim <junhyukkim38@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: defer probe before touching the hardware

airoha_probe() programs the frame engine and both QDMA blocks in
airoha_hw_init(), including the AN7563 hardware forwarding queues and
their DRAM pool, and only afterwards allocates the GDM ports. A port
that reads its MAC address from an nvmem cell gets -EPROBE_DEFER while
the flash provider has not probed yet. On the Xiaomi BE5000 this
happens on every boot, so the driver probes twice and initialises the
frame engine and QDMA twice.

The failure path stops the QDMA TX/RX DMA but resets neither QDMA nor
the frame engine, and the hardware forwarding descriptors and buffers,
allocated with dmam_alloc_coherent(), are released while
REG_FWD_DSCP_BASE and REG_FWD_BUF_BASE still point at them, until the
next probe programs the queues again.

Look up the MAC address of every available eth-mac node before the
hardware is set up and return -EPROBE_DEFER from there, so that a
deferred probe leaves the hardware untouched. With this the BE5000
probes once and the PSE setup runs once per boot.

Tested on the Xiaomi BE5000: probe runs once, MAC addresses unchanged.
The wan_qos upload stall from #10 is not affected.

Signed-off-by: Jun-Hyuk Kim <junhyukkim38@gmail.com>
Link: https://github.com/pabragin/xiaomi-be5000-openwrt/issues/10
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
@@ -5157,6 +5157,23 @@
 		return err;
 	}
 
+	/* The GDM ports may read their MAC address from an nvmem cell whose
+	 * provider is not there yet. Defer before the hardware is set up, so
+	 * that a deferred probe leaves the frame engine and QDMA untouched.
+	 */
+	for_each_available_child_of_node(pdev->dev.of_node, np) {
+		u8 addr[ETH_ALEN];
+
+		if (!of_device_is_compatible(np, "airoha,eth-mac"))
+			continue;
+
+		if (of_get_mac_address(np, addr) == -EPROBE_DEFER) {
+			of_node_put(np);
+			return dev_err_probe(eth->dev, -EPROBE_DEFER,
+					     "MAC address not available yet\n");
+		}
+	}
+
 	eth->napi_dev = alloc_netdev_dummy(0);
 	if (!eth->napi_dev)
 		return -ENOMEM;
